  "query_preview": "..\n(Today through Friday)\nIssued at 101 AM CDT SAT OCT 10 2026\n\nA busy night is unfolding across Central Alabama as Hurricane\nIsaias continues to move north across the state. The system made\nlandfall at 8:30 PM near Destin, FL with max sustained winds of\n105 MPH. The system has gradually weakened as it makes its way\nfurther inland. However, this weakening will lead to an expanding\nwind field across most of Central Alabama. Latest hi-res guidance\ncontinues to suggest swaths of 50-60 MPH winds through mid\nmorning. Our other concern this morning is our heavy rainfall\nthreat. MRMS instantaneous rainfall rates with some of the\nheavier activity are ranging from 3-4\"/hr. At this point, we are\nstill expecting 2-4\" of rain with isolated higher amounts. We are\ncurrently not planning on any changes to our watch/warning\nconfiguration with the 1 AM update. We will reevaluate the\nsituation through  the morning and start clearing counties from\nthe tropical products suite.\n\nA decaying Isaias quickly peels off to the northeast late this\nweekend as upper level ridging builds into the region. This will\nlead to a stretch of warm and dry conditions with highs settling\ninto the mid to upper 80s daily. Mornings look nice with lows in\nthe mid to upper\n\n95/Castillo",
